---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { existsSync } from "node:fs";
import { PATHS, waysPhrase, DOOR_WORD } from "../data/paths.js";
import { INSTRUMENTS, SEEKING, AUTHORED, BASE, seekingPhrase } from "../data/instruments.js";
const N = INSTRUMENTS.length;

// Card art is the case's own tile: a purpose-drawn schematic animated with the phenomenon
// the instrument measures — sensors reporting, ground settling, fire spreading, a story
// travelling. Kept as separate .svg files rather than inlined, because they carry real
// geographic path data (air-quality alone is 176 KB of kelurahan boundaries) and ten of
// those in one document would be most of a megabyte. As files they animate under <img>,
// cache on their own, stay crisp at any size and need no JavaScript. Two cases ship a
// raster tile, so which ones animate is read off the filesystem rather than hand-listed.
const tileFor = (slug) => {
  for (const ext of ["svg", "jpg"]) {
    if (existsSync(new URL(`../../public/tiles/${slug}.${ext}`, import.meta.url)))
      return { src: `/tiles/${slug}.${ext}`, animated: ext === "svg" };
  }
  return null;
};
const tiles = Object.fromEntries(INSTRUMENTS.map((i) => [i.slug, tileFor(i.slug)]));
const nAnimated = Object.values(tiles).filter((t) => t?.animated).length;


---

<Page
  title="Advanced analytics and dashboards for research that already exists"
  description="Your research, rebuilt as a working instrument: a pipeline that re-runs from source, models scored against honest baselines, and an interactive dashboard you can present. Advanced analytics, ML/AI and advanced visualization on published or in-progress work. The findings stay yours."
  current="/"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Open data · Indonesia — advanced analytics · ML/AI · interactive dashboards</p>
    <h1>You did the research.<br /><em>It still lives in a PDF.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      There is usually code behind a paper — a script, a solver, a model that works. What
      there usually is not is a pipeline anyone else can re-run from source, an evaluation
      against baselines that already contain the easy knowledge, and an <b>interactive
      dashboard you can put on a screen in front of a room</b>. That is what gets built
      here, on work you have already published or are still working on.
      <b>Advanced analytics and advanced visualization are the missing parts — not your
      expertise.</b> The findings stay yours.
    </p>
    <dl class="tally">
      <div><dt>Instruments live</dt><dd>{N}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Open to a domain author</dt><dd class="want">{SEEKING.length}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Approached so far</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Open to challenge</dt><dd>{N}</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="tally-note">
      <b>Nought conclusions is not nought refusals.</b> No domain author has been
      approached yet — the invitations are written and unsent. Four readers told us the old
      wording read as ten people having already said no, so it is stated here instead of
      left to be guessed.
    </p>

    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="cta" href="#instruments">
        {SEEKING.length > 0
          ? `${SEEKING.length} instrument${SEEKING.length === 1 ? "" : "s"} seeking an author`
          : `${N} instruments, all open to challenge`}
      </a>
      <a class="cta ghost" href="/contribute/">How this works</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="caught">
    <div class="rail">Analysis<span>what drawing cannot do</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <h2>The findings a dashboard could never have produced</h2>
      <p>
        Visualization is the last of four stages and the most visible, which makes it easy
        to mistake for the whole job. It is not. Every result below came out of the
        pipeline, the estimator or the evaluation — and each one contradicted something the
        instrument was expected to show.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What the analysis caught</th><th>Where</th><th>Why it took analysis</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Recovered a compressor efficiency its own author no longer had.</b>
                A 2016 paper reported a COP but not the isentropic efficiency behind it.
                Inverting the cycle against three different refrigerant mixtures returned
                0.642, 0.649 and 0.662 — a spread of 0.01</td>
              <td>cascade replication</td>
              <td>Three independent fluids agreeing is the evidence. A wrong cycle would
                have scattered</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>57% of the squared error was a single constant per province.</b>
                A satellite cannot see the poverty line the roofs are judged against —
                and a random k-fold would have reported R² 0.65 instead of 0.395</td>
              <td>poverty map</td>
              <td>Only a spatially-blocked fold design exposes it. The inflation was the
                finding</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>A nowcast 41% worse than doing nothing</b>, with 99.5% of it
                intercept — a number that would print almost unchanged if the satellite
                were switched off</td>
              <td>nightlights</td>
              <td>Requires scoring against a naive rival. Without one it looks like skill</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The archive shrank 4.4× in nine years.</b> Raw counts would have told
                a confident and completely false story about attention</td>
              <td>global narrative</td>
              <td>Found by checking the denominator, which nothing in the data announces</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Precision bought by detecting less.</b> A detector quoted 83%
                agreement as evidence it found the rice; precision is the one statistic a
                detector can always buy by finding fewer fields</td>
              <td>rice security</td>
              <td>Caught in adversarial review of our own published claim, which was then
                corrected on the page</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="dim">
        Statistics, ML/AI, thermodynamics, spatial validation, and enough software
        engineering to make it re-runnable. The dashboard is what you see; it is not what
        makes the number trustworthy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Why<span>the gap this fills</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>The analysis is the reusable part, and it is the part that gets lost</h2>
      <p>
        A paper's analysis is usually its most reusable part and its least accessible.
        The figures are flattened into a PDF, the pipeline lives on one laptop, and the
        question a reader wants to ask — <em>what happens if I change this assumption?</em>
        — often cannot be asked.
      </p>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>This section used to say "Journals publish figures. Nobody publishes
          instruments." That was false</b>, and three readers said so. JOSS refereeing
          software, ReScience publishing replications, eLife's executable articles, Code
          Ocean capsules and ACM artifact badges all exist. So do OpenSees, OpenFOAM,
          HEC-RAS and SWMM — whole fields have shipped runnable models for decades.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The honest version is narrower. Repositories archive code and workflow engines run
        it — Snakemake, Nextflow, Binder, Quarto, Zenodo, and they are better at those jobs
        than anything built here. What is still uncommon is a <b>single artifact</b> that
        carries the pipeline, the evaluation and an interface a non-specialist can use, for
        a specific finding, in a field where nobody has built one. That is a narrower gap
        than the sentence it replaces, and it is the real one.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p><b>So the work is split by what it takes, not by who owns it.</b> Measurement
        needs statistics, models and software. Explanation needs the subject. Both get
        named credit for what they did, and neither has a veto over the other.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="present">
    <div class="rail">Present it<span>the immediate use</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <h2>Something to stand in front of</h2>
      <p>
        A conference slide can show a result. It cannot answer the question that follows it
        from the third row — <em>what happens if you change that assumption?</em> An
        instrument can, live, while you are still standing there.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What the instrument does that a slide cannot</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Conference talk</b></td>
              <td>Take a question from the floor and answer it by moving a control, at the
                resolution your data actually has</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Thesis defence</b></td>
              <td>Show the examiners the sensitivity they are about to ask about, including
                where the result stops holding</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Grant panel or review</b></td>
              <td>Demonstrate that the pipeline re-runs from raw source — reproducibility as
                a thing you show, not a paragraph you promise</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Seminar or ministry briefing</b></td>
              <td>Let a non-specialist reach their own province, kabupaten or unit and see
                their own number</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>A link in the paper</b></td>
              <td>Readers interrogate the analysis instead of squinting at a flattened
                figure. Supplementary material that is actually used</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>This is the lowest-risk way in.</b> Your paper is already published, the
          findings and their interpretation stay yours, there is no authorship to negotiate,
          and nothing about your record changes. What changes is what you can show.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="built">
    <div class="rail">Built<span>what an instrument is</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Four stages, and you can ask for any of them</h2>
      <p>
        "Instrument" means all four together, versioned and re-runnable from raw source —
        that is the full build. But <b>they are separable</b>, and most requests will not be
        for all four. <b>Visualization is one item on this list, not the list.</b>
      </p>
      <ol class="stages">
        <li>
          <h3>Pipeline</h3>
          <p>Raw open data to analysis-ready tables, idempotent and re-runnable, with the
          fetch, the transforms and the provenance all recorded.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Models — statistical and ML/AI</h3>
          <p>Whichever the problem actually needs, not whichever is fashionable: small-area
          estimation, spatial cross-validation, gradient boosting, time-series nowcasts. A
          model only earns its place by beating the naive rival at stage three.
          <b>None of these methods is new</b> — Fay–Herriot is 1979, gradient boosting is
          2001, blocked spatial validation is standard practice. Using the ordinary method
          correctly, and proving it beat the obvious rival, is the whole job.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Evaluation</h3>
          <p>Scored against a naive rival that already contains the easy knowledge, with
          thresholds fixed <em>before</em> results and every gate published, failures included.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Advanced visualization</h3>
          <p>An interactive dashboard, not a static figure: linked views, animated time,
          drill-down to the unit of analysis, and controls that let a reader change an
          assumption and watch the answer move. Built to be read by someone who did not
          build it — and it is usually the only part a non-specialist will ever use.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Ask for just this</th><th>What you get</th><th>Typical reason</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>The pipeline</b></td>
              <td>Your analysis re-runs from raw source on a machine that is not yours,
                idempotent, provenance recorded</td>
              <td>A reviewer asked for reproducibility, or the only working copy is on one
                laptop</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>The model</b></td>
              <td>Estimator chosen and defended, tuned and scored — statistical or ML/AI,
                whichever the problem actually needs</td>
              <td>The question outgrew the method available when you started</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>The evaluation</b></td>
              <td>Baselines that already contain the easy knowledge, leakage checks, a fold
                design that does not flatter the model, thresholds fixed before results</td>
              <td>You suspect the result is better than it should be, and would rather know
                before a referee does</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>A replication</b></td>
              <td>Your published method rebuilt independently from the paper alone, and a
                straight answer on what reproduces and what does not</td>
              <td>Someone questioned the result — or you want to know first. Needs no data
                and no code from you</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>The visualization</b></td>
              <td>An interactive dashboard to the <a href="/standards/#visualization">six
                published criteria</a> — linked views, navigable time, drill-down, a control
                on an assumption</td>
              <td>You have something to present, and a static figure cannot answer a
                question from the floor</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="dim">
        Each card is that case's own tile, and {nAnimated} of the {N} animate with the
        phenomenon the instrument measures — sensors reporting, ground settling, fire
        spreading, a story travelling. They are schematics, not screenshots: click one for
        the dashboard itself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">How<span>what each side brings</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>How a case comes together — when the analysis is designed here</h2>
      <p class="dim">
        This describes two of the six ways in: interpreting one of the instruments below,
        and starting a new study. On a build over an <b>already-published paper</b> there is
        no split to make — the analysis is yours, the instrument is the addition, and
        <a href="/contribute/#credit">credit says so per path</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Method.</b> Which estimator, which baseline, what would count as
                working — decided and written down before anything runs</td>
              <td><b>The question,</b> and why it matters where it is asked</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Models.</b> Built, tuned and scored — statistical or ML/AI, whichever
                the problem actually needs</td>
              <td><b>Domain knowledge.</b> What a result means, what would be surprising,
                what the literature already settled</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Evaluation.</b> Against naive rivals that already contain the easy
                knowledge, with every gate published including the failures</td>
              <td><b>Plausible mechanism.</b> Why the relationship would exist, or why it
                would not</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The instrument.</b> Pipeline, models, gates and an interactive dashboard — all
                re-runnable, all versioned together</td>
              <td><b>The interpretation,</b> the written discussion, and accountability for
                the finding</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Credit follows the work with <a href="/contribute/#credit">CRediT roles</a>, so
        nobody has to pretend: Methodology, Formal analysis, Validation, Software, Data
        curation and Visualization on one side of that table; Conceptualization,
        Investigation and Writing on the other. Whoever did a thing is credited for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        An instrument is published as soon as the left column is finished, without waiting
        for the right one. Both halves stay open to anyone who can fill them — a domain
        researcher most obviously, but equally someone who can improve a model or break an
        evaluation. A conclusion arriving does not close the left column.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="who">
    <div class="rail">Ways in<span>find your row</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <h2>{waysPhrase()}</h2>
      <p>
        Routed by what you already have, not by job title — a quantitative economist is a
        researcher and a data scientist both, and picking one would be a fiction. Capacity
        is stated honestly next to each, because one person cannot service {DOOR_WORD[PATHS.length] ?? PATHS.length} doors.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you already have</th><th>The path</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {PATHS.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{p.have}<span class="path-note">{p.note}</span></td>
                <td><a href={`/contribute/#${p.anchor}`}>{p.path}</a></td>
                <td><span class={p.status === "open" ? "chip open" : "chip later"}>{p.status}</span></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="sides">
        <div class="side good">
          <h3>What you get</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A <b>working instrument</b> for your subject — pipeline, models, evaluation
              and an interactive dashboard — built free</li>
            <li><b>First author</b> if you write the interpretation. The finding is yours</li>
            <li>You <b>keep copyright</b> and can republish anywhere</li>
            <li>A <b>DOI</b> on request — archived independently, citable, versioned</li>
            <li><b>Named CRediT credit</b> for whatever you actually did, not a thank-you line</li>
            <li>Thresholds fixed before results, so the evaluation is defensible to a reviewer</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="side warn">
          <h3>What you should know</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><b>Not peer reviewed.</b> Gates are checking, not refereeing</li>
            <li>This site has <b>no audience yet.</b> You are betting it gets one</li>
            <li>Publishing unpublished work here first makes it a <b>preprint</b> — check
              your journal's policy. This does not apply to an already-published paper</li>
            <li><b>Failed gates are published.</b> If the measurement contradicts the
              finding, the page says so</li>
            <li>Implementation is <b>AI-assisted</b>, disclosed on every instrument</li>
            <li>One person, one server, no grant. <b>It could stop.</b></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="panel-next">
        Each path in full, including the two that need asking first —
        <a href="/contribute/">how to contribute</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="instruments">
    <div class="rail">Instruments<span>{seekingPhrase()}</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <h2>{N} instruments, and what each is still open to</h2>
      <p>
        Every one is complete as measurement: the pipeline runs, the gates are published
        including the failures, the interactive dashboard works. What is open differs by instrument —
        and <b>nothing here ever closes.</b>
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Open to</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="chip">seeking author</span></td>
              <td>No conclusion written yet</td>
              <td>A domain author, for first authorship — <b>and</b> anyone who can improve
                a model or break the evaluation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="chip done">authored</span></td>
              <td>A domain author has written the conclusion and holds first authorship</td>
              <td>Challenge only: a stronger baseline, a flaw in the evaluation, newer data.
                A successful challenge publishes as its own finding</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <ul class="cards">
        {INSTRUMENTS.map((i) => (
          <li class="card">
            {/* aria-hidden and tabindex -1 because the title link beside it goes to the
                same place, and a screen reader should not hear one target twice. */}
            <a class="card-shot" href={`${BASE}/${i.slug}/`} aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">
              <img src={tiles[i.slug]?.src ?? `/shots/${i.slug}.jpg`} alt=""
                   width="400" height="240" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            </a>
            <div class="card-body">
            <div class="card-head">
              <a class="card-title" href={`${BASE}/${i.slug}/`}>
                {i.title} <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              </a>
              {i.status === "authored"
                ? <span class="chip done">authored · open to challenge</span>
                : <span class="chip">seeking author</span>}
            </div>
            <p class="card-line">{i.line}</p>
            <p class="card-found"><b>What it found.</b> {i.found}</p>
            {i.status === "authored"
              ? <p class="card-gap"><b>Interpretation.</b> {i.author} — and the measurement
                  remains open to challenge.</p>
              : <p class="card-gap"><b>What is missing.</b> {i.gap}</p>}
            <p class="card-links">
              <a href={`${BASE}/${i.slug}/`}>Open the instrument</a>
              <a href={`${BASE}/${i.slug}/article/`}>Read the write-up</a>
            </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          If one of these is your subject, the measurement is already done. Writing the
          explanation earns <b>first author</b>; the measurement earns <b>methods
          co-author</b>. If instead you can see something wrong with how one was measured,
          that is a contribution too — see <a href="/contribute/">how to contribute</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="dim">Read <a href="/contribute/">how to contribute</a> first — including the parts that might put you off.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Page>

<style>
  .hero { padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .hero h1 { max-width: 26ch; }
  .hero-actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem; margin-top: 1.8rem; }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.82rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em; text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.05rem; border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground); background: var(--accent);
  }
  .cta.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--accent); }
  .cta { box-shadow: 0 0 28px -10px var(--glow); }
  .cta:hover {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 88%, var(--ink)); color: var(--ground);
    box-shadow: 0 0 34px -6px var(--glow);
  }
  .cta.ghost:hover { background: var(--panel); color: var(--accent); }

  .sec .col.wide { max-width: none; }

  /* A real sequence — each stage consumes the previous one's output — so the numbers
     carry information rather than decorating. */
  .stages { list-style: none; counter-reset: st; margin: 1.4rem 0 1.2rem; padding: 0; }
  .stages > li {
    counter-increment: st; position: relative;
    padding: 0 0 0 3.1rem; margin-bottom: 1.3rem;
  }
  .stages > li::before {
    content: counter(st, decimal-leading-zero);
    position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0.05rem;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.82rem; letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--accent); text-shadow: 0 0 16px var(--glow);
  }
  .stages > li::after {
    content: ""; position: absolute; left: 1.05rem; top: 1.6rem; bottom: -1.3rem;
    width: 1px; background: var(--rule);
  }
  .stages > li:last-child::after { display: none; }
  .stages h3 { margin: 0 0 0.25rem; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .stages > li:last-child h3 { color: var(--accent); text-shadow: 0 0 20px var(--glow); }
  .stages p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; }

  .tally {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(9.5rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1px; margin: 1.9rem 0 0; padding: 0;
    background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .tally > div { background: var(--ground); padding: 0.85rem 1rem; }
  .tally dt {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
  }
  .tally dd {
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.7rem;
    font-weight: 600; line-height: 1; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .tally dd.want { color: var(--flag); text-shadow: 0 0 22px var(--glow-flag); }
  .tally-note {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--muted); margin: 0.9rem 0 0; max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .tally-note b { color: var(--ink); }

  .cards { list-style: none; margin: 1.5rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .card {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 1.4rem 0;
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 19rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 1.6rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .card:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); }
